---
/**
 * Homepage (Phase 2D). Lifecycle: published_noindex.
 *
 * States what the resource is, who it helps, and where the work currently stands. It makes no
 * claim about market position, project counts, contractor networks, homeowner outcomes, savings,
 * or response times, because none of those exist. There is no lead form and no quote CTA:
 * live intake is disabled and collecting homeowner information is out of scope
 * (docs/05 → Lead Funnel; /AGENTS.md §4.1, §4.2).
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout pageId="home" isSiteRoot>
  <div class="prose prose--wide">
    <h1>Sewer decisions, explained with the rules that actually apply</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Indy Sewer Resource is an independent resource for Indianapolis-area homeowners facing a sewer
      lateral problem: backups that keep coming back, a camera inspection they did not expect, or a
      repair quote they do not know how to read. We read the municipal rules, cite them, and say plainly
      what is still unknown.
    </p>

    <h2 id="start-here">Start here</h2>
    <ul class="cards">
      <li class="card">
        <h3><a href="/lawrence-sewer-lateral-repair/">Sewer lateral repair in Lawrence, Indiana</a></h3>
        <p>
          Who owns the pipe, the permit the City requires, what a camera inspection settles, which
          repair methods Lawrence allows, and the video the utility requires before trenchless work is
          approved — each point cited to the ordinance or the utility's own documents.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3><a href="/about/">How this resource works</a></h3>
        <p>
          Where our information comes from, how we separate verified facts from reasoning, when a page
          needs professional review, and how we correct things. Also: what we will disclose if a
          contractor ever sponsors this site.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="what-this-is">What this is</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Most sewer information a homeowner finds online is written by a company that wants to sell the
        repair, or it is national content that ignores the local rules deciding what is even allowed on
        your street. Both leave the same questions unanswered: is this pipe mine, what am I required to
        do, and what should the work in front of me actually include?
      </p>
      <p>
        This resource works the other way around. It starts from municipal ordinances, utility policy
        manuals, and the utility's own homeowner guidance, cites each one, and flags the places where
        the public record does not answer the question.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="decisions">The decisions this covers</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <strong>The problem.</strong> Repeated backups, slow drains, wet spots, or a camera finding
          you did not expect.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Diagnosis.</strong> What a camera inspection can and cannot settle before you spend
          money.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Responsibility.</strong> Which part of the line is yours, and which belongs to the
          utility — under the rules for your municipality, not a general answer.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The repair-method decision.</strong> How spot repair, full-length lining, pipe
          bursting, and open-cut replacement differ, and which ones your municipality permits. Which
          one suits your line is a contractor's judgment, not ours.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The quote.</strong> What to check a quote addresses — the permit, the inspection, and
          the post-repair verification the municipality requires. We publish no prices and no view on
          whether a given quote is reasonable; we have no local price evidence.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The contractor.</strong> What to ask, and what the municipality requires of whoever
          does the work.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <h2 id="scope">Where this applies</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The scope is the Indianapolis metro. The first municipality covered in depth is
        <a href="/lawrence-sewer-lateral-repair/">Lawrence</a>, because its rules are published and
        verifiable and its homeowners face the full range of lateral decisions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Other municipalities are added only when there is enough verified local evidence to be useful.
        A page that simply swaps a city name into the same text helps nobody, and this project does not
        publish them.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="status">Where this project currently stands</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Being straight about the state of the work:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The Lawrence resource is a <strong>pre-publication draft</strong> awaiting review by a qualified sewer professional.</li>
        <li>No page is published to search engines yet.</li>
        <li>No contractor partner is active, and no contractor pays for placement here.</li>
        <li>No homeowner information is collected on this site: there is no form, and no data has ever been gathered.</li>
        <li>There are no reviews, ratings, testimonials, or project statistics here, because none exist.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If a contractor relationship is established later, it will be disclosed where it applies — see
        <a href="/about/">how this resource works</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
